---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import Crt from '../components/Crt.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';
import { APP, AUTHOR, REPO } from '../lib/site';

const halt = `$ cd /
$ sync; sync
$ /etc/halt`;
---

<PageLayout
  title="Support — ipnx"
  description="How to get help with ipnx: where to report a problem, what to include, and answers to the questions a 1985 Unix raises most often."
  eyebrow="Help"
  heading="Support"
  intro="Where to ask, what to include, and the handful of things that surprise everyone the first time."
>
  <div class="grid gap-12 py-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_18rem] lg:items-start">
    <Prose>
      <h2>Getting help</h2>
      <p>
        The best place is <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>GitHub Issues</a>, because the answer is then
        readable by the next person with the same problem. If you would rather write privately,
        <a href={`mailto:${AUTHOR.email}`}>{AUTHOR.email}</a> reaches the same person.
      </p>
      <p>Whatever you use, it helps enormously to include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your macOS version and Mac model.</li>
        <li>
          The {APP.name} version, from <strong>ipnx → About</strong>, and what the emulated machine
          says when you run <code>ipnxfetch</code>.
        </li>
        <li>What you did, what happened, and what you expected instead.</li>
        <li>
          A screenshot if anything looked wrong. A photograph of a screen is fine too — this is a
          project about terminals.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Things that surprise people</h2>

      <h3>Never force-quit it</h3>
      <p>
        This is the one rule that matters. The emulated machine has a filesystem with data in
        memory that has not reached the disk, exactly as a real one does, and killing the app is
        the equivalent of pulling the plug on a VAX. Quitting normally is safe — ⌘Q snapshots the
        running machine and resumes it next time. If you want the machine properly shut down,
        type this into a shell first:
      </p>
      <Crt text={halt} caption="What a 1985 operator would have typed before going home." />
      <p>
        If it is ever force-quit anyway, the next launch runs a filesystem check and repairs
        itself. That usually works. <strong>Usually</strong> is doing real work in that sentence:
        <code>fsck</code> restores consistency, not data.
      </p>

      <h3>The keyboard seems dead after resuming</h3>
      <p>
        Press <kbd>Return</kbd>. A restored session has nothing left to say — the login prompt was
        printed once, before the snapshot — so the screen can look alive and silent at the same
        time. The app sends that nudge for you, but if a window looks frozen, Return is the first
        thing to try.
      </p>

      <h3>Backspace, and other 1985 opinions</h3>
      <p>
        The Delete key sends <kbd>^H</kbd>, which is what Eighth Edition expects. Interrupt is
        <kbd>^C</kbd> and line-kill is <kbd>^U</kbd>. There is no command history and no
        completion; <code>sh</code> in 1985 had neither.
      </p>

      <h3>File names are short, and case matters</h3>
      <p>
        Fourteen characters, maximum, and no exceptions. This is why your Mac home folder is
        mounted at <code>/n/home</code> rather than being used as the account's home directory —
        the emulated Unix simply cannot represent many of the names your Mac contains.
      </p>

      <h3>It is using CPU while apparently idle</h3>
      <p>
        Two processors are running. The VAX idles properly and costs very little; the 5620
        terminal does not idle at all, because a real one did not. Closing the 5620 window stops
        it and gives the core back.
      </p>

      <h2>Something is genuinely broken</h2>
      <p>
        Start again from a clean machine: <strong>Settings → Disk → Reset</strong> replaces the
        emulated disk with the one the app shipped. This <em>erases everything inside the emulated
        machine</em> — your files there, not on your Mac — so export anything you want first.
        Files on shared folders are on your Mac and are untouched.
      </p>

      <h2>Feature requests and the rest of the project</h2>
      <p>
        Very welcome, with one caveat worth stating early: {APP.name} is a restoration, so
        "Eighth Edition does not do that" is sometimes the correct and final answer. The
        interesting question is usually whether the real system could have done it. See the
        <a href={href('/about')}>project notes</a> for where this is going.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <aside class="border border-rule bg-card p-6 lg:sticky lg:top-8">
      <p class="font-sans text-[12px] font-bold tracking-[0.1em] text-muted uppercase">
        Quick links
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 font-sans text-[14px]">
        <li>
          <a href={`${REPO}/issues`} class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-emphasis">
            Report an issue ↗
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={href('/manual')} class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-emphasis">
            The manual
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={href('/manual/troubleshooting')} class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-emphasis">
            Troubleshooting
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={href('/privacy')} class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-emphasis">
            Privacy
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 border-t border-rule pt-4 font-sans text-[13px] text-muted">
        This is a personal project maintained by one person in their own time. Answers may take a
        few days; they will come.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 font-sans text-[13px]">
        <a href={`mailto:${AUTHOR.email}`} class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-emphasis">
          {AUTHOR.email}
        </a>
      </p>
    </aside>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
